<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<!--
  THE LOCAL-DEVELOPMENT APP TRANSPORT SECURITY EXCEPTION, KEPT OUT OF THE
  SHIPPED PLIST.

  This file is NOT an Info.plist and is NOT copied into any bundle. It is a
  fragment: the `Verify Packaged Bundle` build phase merges it into the
  packaged Info.plist of a Debug build compiled for LOCAL, and does nothing
  with it in every other case. A DEV, STAGING or PRODUCTION artifact therefore
  has no NSAppTransportSecurity key at all rather than a switched-off one.

  Keeping it in its own file is what makes the scope visible. The previous
  arrangement put this dictionary in Runner/Info.plist with a comment
  explaining that iOS builds one plist for all configurations and that the
  exception was consequently present in release binaries. That explanation was
  accurate and the outcome was still wrong: a reviewer, a store submission and
  anyone unpacking the artifact all read the plist, not the comment.

  WHY THE EXCEPTION EXISTS AT ALL. App Transport Security applies to loopback
  connections, and the LOCAL profile runs the API on the machine of the
  developer at http://localhost:3000 — the same address the Dart configuration
  loader uses as its LOCAL default.

  WHY `localhost` AND NOT AN IP LITERAL. ATS exception domains are matched as
  domain names, so an entry for 127.0.0.1 would never take effect. iOS local
  development uses the name.

  NSAllowsArbitraryLoads is stated as false rather than omitted, because here
  there is a dictionary to state it in and an exception beside it to scope. The
  platform default is already false, so the line changes no behaviour; it
  exists so that a future "just for now" change to true is a visible edit to an
  explicit decision instead of the addition of a missing key. The blanket keys
  NSAllowsArbitraryLoadsInWebContent, NSAllowsArbitraryLoadsForMedia and
  NSAllowsLocalNetworking are absent, and the build phase fails the build if
  any of them reaches a packaged artifact from any source.
-->
<dict>
	<key>NSAllowsArbitraryLoads</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSExceptionDomains</key>
	<dict>
		<key>localhost</key>
		<dict>
			<key>NSExceptionAllowsInsecureHTTPLoads</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSIncludesSubdomains</key>
			<false/>
		</dict>
	</dict>
</dict>
</plist>
